    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "1dbf12ac-cc61-3661-913e-719c971d918d",
                "word": "grogram",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "91f9202b-33a0-4099-850b-6ea63ecfcde4",
                        "entry_id": "1dbf12ac-cc61-3661-913e-719c971d918d",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "grograms"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "47bf88a5-510b-3cc3-b9ba-d76678184414",
                        "entry_id": "1dbf12ac-cc61-3661-913e-719c971d918d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A strong, rough fabric made up of a mixture of silk, and mohair or wool."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "69f41ad4-5b02-460a-baff-66f13f93b51b",
                                "sense_id": "47bf88a5-510b-3cc3-b9ba-d76678184414",
                                "text": "I like ſome humors of the Cittie Dames well: to eate Cherries onely at an Angell a pound, good; to dye rich Scarlet black, pretty: to line a Grogaram gowne cleane thorough with veluet, tollerable; their pure linnen, their ſmocks of 3. li. a ſmock are to be borne withall. But your minſing nicetyes, taffata pipkins, durance petticotes & ſilver bodkins—Gods my life, as I ſhall be a Lady, I cannot indure it."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4d8ea71e-29bc-437b-bde1-cc061397965c",
                                "sense_id": "47bf88a5-510b-3cc3-b9ba-d76678184414",
                                "text": "Are not your Frenchmen neate? Fine, as you ſee, / I have but one frenchman, looke, hee followes mee. / Certes they are neatly cloth'd. I, of this minde am, / Your only wearing is your Grogaram; / Not ſo Sir, I have more."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5bd110ed-ddf5-4013-9c38-991c2b4cfb9c",
                                "sense_id": "47bf88a5-510b-3cc3-b9ba-d76678184414",
                                "text": "Tell him, if he will, / He ſhall ha' the grogran's, at the rate I told him, / And I will meet him, on the Exchange, anon."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "46bb1441-5b85-4e45-8768-b051b402e2a1",
                                "sense_id": "47bf88a5-510b-3cc3-b9ba-d76678184414",
                                "text": "The merchants aduise mee, that there is intended a proclamation for the prohibition of grograms, which, if it may aduance our owne commodity, will be an act of good policy; but I am bound to informe, it will retrench halfe the trade of this port."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3e853dc7-8d13-484c-a076-e02a6223618a",
                                "sense_id": "47bf88a5-510b-3cc3-b9ba-d76678184414",
                                "text": "Inſtead of Home-ſpun quoifs were ſeen / Good Pinners, edg'd with Colberteen: / Her Petticoats tra[n]sform'd apace, / Became Black Satin flounc'd with Lace. / Plain Goody would no longer down, / 'Twas Madam in her Grogram gown."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8ef8edc2-e52b-4b2e-b348-2049581d69fc",
                                "sense_id": "47bf88a5-510b-3cc3-b9ba-d76678184414",
                                "text": "The natural sweetness and innocence of her behaviour, the freshness of her complexion, the unaffected turn of her shape and person, shot me through and through every time I saw her, and did more execution upon me in grogram, than the greatest beauty in town or court had ever done in brocade."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b1576eae-2a32-44b3-a511-e5f15e997934",
                                "sense_id": "47bf88a5-510b-3cc3-b9ba-d76678184414",
                                "text": "[W]e shall have petulance and inattention, instead of bashful civility, because it is the fashion with fine folks to be easy; and rusticity shall be set off with impudence, like a grogram waistcoat with tinsel binding, that only makes its coarseness more disgusting."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2f7e7582-4304-409b-af55-c78efd6c78b2",
                                "sense_id": "47bf88a5-510b-3cc3-b9ba-d76678184414",
                                "text": "Her mother [...] sat by the fire in the full glory of a grogram gown, lammer beads, and a clean cockernony, whiffing a snug pipe of tobacco, and superintending the affairs of the kitchen."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2d1a177b-5629-4805-8562-57eb865096c2",
                                "sense_id": "47bf88a5-510b-3cc3-b9ba-d76678184414",
                                "text": "An' here, they'n gi'en you lots o' good grogram an' flannel, as should ha' been gi'en by good rights to them as had the sense to keep away from such foolery. Ye might spare me a bit o' this grogram to make clothes for the lad—ye war ne'er ill-natur'd, Bess; I ne'er said that on ye."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "740b7bf2-f3d6-40b1-932f-a6d499c6a479",
                                "sense_id": "47bf88a5-510b-3cc3-b9ba-d76678184414",
                                "text": "The daily diet consisted of cheese, tough beef preserved in salt, pork, biscuits and half a pint of 'grog'. This was rum diluted with water to reduce its potency, as dictated by Admiral [Edward] Vernon back in 1740. Nicknamed 'Old Grogram' because of the grogram waterproof he so often wore, the rum ration took his nickname also."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4bcb38f8-3200-4376-b813-1d4e7636e210",
                                "sense_id": "47bf88a5-510b-3cc3-b9ba-d76678184414",
                                "text": "They had known nothing of woolen cloth, but now the popularity of obi made of imported grogram spread like wildfire. This popularity produced various stories in its wake."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9d9c9056-9b71-3aa9-8f5b-b2e26c883cbd",
                        "entry_id": "1dbf12ac-cc61-3661-913e-719c971d918d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A garment made from this fabric."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "eb637007-1e50-4778-8505-3d75da21bf65",
                                "sense_id": "9d9c9056-9b71-3aa9-8f5b-b2e26c883cbd",
                                "text": "[W]ould you, Lady Alma, refuse to purchase a new gown, when by length of time your old grogram was worn to tatters, or grown so unfashionable as to excite ridicule in the very boys as you go to church?"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "6ff1b6b4-9eaf-42b7-b7a2-ce388965e1ad",
                        "source_entry_id": "1dbf12ac-cc61-3661-913e-719c971d918d",
                        "source_sense_description": "strong, rough fabric made up of a mixture of silk, and mohair or wool",
                        "target_lang_word": "gourgouran",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "gourgouran",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1c430d3b-cf3a-38dc-b2f1-7e6e5a91a20e",
                                        "entry_id": "e4751984-ea3a-3ab2-a822-3c37ce746b21",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Étoffe de soie, brochée, originaire de l’Inde, et analogue au gros de Tours. Elle à des bandes de différentes couleurs ou nuances unies, faites selon deux armures différentes."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6911ce7c-c216-4d48-b610-f8b905ef7ac3",
                                                "sense_id": "1c430d3b-cf3a-38dc-b2f1-7e6e5a91a20e",
                                                "text": "Je passe dans la chambre voisine, qui est drapée d'un gourgouran du vert le plus doux qui se puisse imaginer."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "58d29046-b8fc-41f0-b290-08f09be13309",
                                                "sense_id": "1c430d3b-cf3a-38dc-b2f1-7e6e5a91a20e",
                                                "text": "Les tribunaux frappèrent, tout d’abord, assez durement, les gentilles délinquantes qui s’obstinaient à porter le gourgouran, soie légère de l’Inde, l’armoisin, joli taffetas très souple et le cirsac mollement cotonneux."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
